The form:

```go
func divide(a, b int) (int, error) {
    if b == 0 {
        return 0, fmt.Errorf("division by zero")
    }
    return a / b, nil
}

q, err := divide(10, 2)   // 5, nil
q, err = divide(10, 0)    // 0, error
```

Executed. The convention the whole ecosystem follows: error is the LAST return value; on failure, the other values are zero/nil (don't trust them); on success, error is nil. Check err before using the values.
